1
00:00:00,400 --> 00:00:05,240
You could call Voyager 1 today, but your
hello would take a full day to

2
00:00:05,260 --> 00:00:06,100
arrive.

3
00:00:06,120 --> 00:00:09,820
And in nineteen seventy-seven, it
almost never left Earth.

4
00:00:09,920 --> 00:00:12,300
Today it's forty-nine years old.

5
00:00:12,320 --> 00:00:15,180
It's running on less than half of
its original power,

6
00:00:15,200 --> 00:00:18,360
and it has two working science
instruments left.

7
00:00:18,380 --> 00:00:20,720
So why don't we just switch it off?

8
00:00:20,820 --> 00:00:23,240
To answer that, you need the whole story.

9
00:00:23,260 --> 00:00:26,040
And it starts with planets lining up.

10
00:00:26,060 --> 00:00:30,840
In the mid nineteen-sixties, a young engineer at
NASA's Jet Propulsion Laboratory,

11
00:00:30,860 --> 00:00:34,600
Gary Flandro, was working out routes
to the outer planets.

12
00:00:34,620 --> 00:00:36,440
He noticed something rare.

13
00:00:36,460 --> 00:00:39,680
In the late nineteen-seventies,
Jupiter, Saturn,

14
00:00:39,700 --> 00:00:43,920
Uranus and Neptune would all sit on
the same side of the Sun,

15
00:00:43,940 --> 00:00:45,930
lined up like stepping stones.

16
00:00:46,020 --> 00:00:49,840
That only happens about once every
hundred and seventy-five years.

17
00:00:49,920 --> 00:00:50,950
Why does that matter?

18
00:00:51,200 --> 00:00:53,990
Because a planet can throw a spacecraft.

19
00:00:54,080 --> 00:00:58,460
Fly close behind Jupiter as it moves, and
its gravity pulls you along and

20
00:00:58,480 --> 00:01:02,300
flings you out faster, like a skateboarder
grabbing the back of a passing

21
00:01:02,320 --> 00:01:03,030
bus.

22
00:01:03,120 --> 00:01:07,790
Planet after planet, each one hands the
spacecraft on to the next.

23
00:01:07,880 --> 00:01:11,990
Without that trick, a trip to Neptune
would take about forty years.

24
00:01:12,040 --> 00:01:13,950
With it, less than ten.

25
00:01:14,220 --> 00:01:17,920
Miss the window, and you wait more
than a century and a half.

26
00:01:18,040 --> 00:01:19,550
NASA didn't miss it.

27
00:01:19,760 --> 00:01:24,750
NASA built two identical spacecraft,
Voyager 1 and Voyager 2.

28
00:01:24,840 --> 00:01:27,420
Each one is about the size of a small car,

29
00:01:27,440 --> 00:01:30,340
with a big white dish antenna
pointed at home.

30
00:01:30,360 --> 00:01:32,820
The official mission was
Jupiter and Saturn,

31
00:01:32,840 --> 00:01:35,580
and it was planned to last
about five years.

32
00:01:35,600 --> 00:01:39,220
Their computers had about
sixty-nine kilobytes of memory.

33
00:01:39,240 --> 00:01:42,180
A single photo on your phone is
bigger than that.

34
00:01:42,200 --> 00:01:44,780
There are no solar panels,
because out there,

35
00:01:44,800 --> 00:01:46,710
sunlight is far too weak.

36
00:01:46,840 --> 00:01:50,100
Instead, each Voyager carries a
nuclear battery.

37
00:01:50,120 --> 00:01:54,080
Plutonium gives off heat, and the heat
is turned into electricity.

38
00:01:54,100 --> 00:01:55,960
It never runs out suddenly.

39
00:01:55,980 --> 00:01:58,220
It just slowly fades.

40
00:01:58,240 --> 00:02:02,300
And bolted to the side, each one
carries a gold-plated record.

41
00:02:02,320 --> 00:02:06,540
A committee led by the astronomer Carl
Sagan filled it with pictures,

42
00:02:06,560 --> 00:02:10,070
sounds of Earth, and greetings in
fifty-five languages.

43
00:02:10,320 --> 00:02:13,540
There's whale song, thunder, a heartbeat.

44
00:02:13,560 --> 00:02:16,900
There's Bach, Beethoven, and
Chuck Berry's Johnny B.

45
00:02:16,920 --> 00:02:17,390
Goode.

46
00:02:17,660 --> 00:02:21,250
Some people on the committee thought rock
and roll was too adolescent.

47
00:02:21,340 --> 00:02:25,010
Sagan's answer was that there are a lot of
adolescents on this planet.

48
00:02:25,160 --> 00:02:26,620
It went on the record.

49
00:02:26,740 --> 00:02:29,850
Just in case someone, someday, finds it.

50
00:02:30,060 --> 00:02:33,040
Voyager 2 launched first, on
August twentieth,

51
00:02:33,060 --> 00:02:34,560
nineteen seventy-seven.

52
00:02:34,580 --> 00:02:37,080
Voyager 1 followed on September fifth.

53
00:02:37,100 --> 00:02:40,640
It got the number one because it
was on a faster path,

54
00:02:40,660 --> 00:02:42,530
and it would reach Jupiter first.

55
00:02:42,840 --> 00:02:45,030
That launch almost went wrong.

56
00:02:45,200 --> 00:02:48,060
One stage of the Titan rocket
underperformed,

57
00:02:48,080 --> 00:02:51,640
so the Centaur upper stage had to burn
longer to make up for it.

58
00:02:51,800 --> 00:02:55,060
When it was done, according to the
mission's planning manager,

59
00:02:55,080 --> 00:02:58,210
it had about three and a half
seconds of fuel left.

60
00:02:58,340 --> 00:03:00,570
Three and a half seconds.

61
00:03:00,700 --> 00:03:03,860
That was the first time Voyager
almost didn't make it.

62
00:03:03,980 --> 00:03:05,250
It wasn't the last.

63
00:03:05,520 --> 00:03:10,470
In April nineteen seventy-eight, Voyager
2's main radio receiver failed.

64
00:03:10,560 --> 00:03:12,830
The backup had a broken part too.

65
00:03:12,920 --> 00:03:17,660
It could still hear Earth, but only on
one very narrow frequency,

66
00:03:17,680 --> 00:03:21,670
and that frequency drifts with the
spacecraft's temperature and motion.

67
00:03:21,760 --> 00:03:24,070
So engineers learned to aim.

68
00:03:24,220 --> 00:03:28,440
For every command since then, they have
worked out exactly where Voyager

69
00:03:28,460 --> 00:03:29,710
2 will be listening.

70
00:03:29,760 --> 00:03:33,540
For forty-eight years, it has been
flying on its backup receiver.

71
00:03:33,680 --> 00:03:37,670
In March nineteen seventy-nine,
Voyager 1 reached Jupiter.

72
00:03:37,860 --> 00:03:41,860
The pictures were stunning:
storms bigger than Earth,

73
00:03:41,880 --> 00:03:45,030
and the Great Red Spot
swirling like paint.

74
00:03:45,120 --> 00:03:48,710
It even found that Jupiter has
thin, faint rings.

75
00:03:48,800 --> 00:03:52,060
But the biggest surprise came from
a navigation engineer,

76
00:03:52,080 --> 00:03:53,370
Linda Morabito.

77
00:03:53,460 --> 00:03:56,560
She was checking images used to
steer the spacecraft,

78
00:03:56,580 --> 00:04:00,790
and she saw a strange cloud at the
edge of Jupiter's moon Io.

79
00:04:00,840 --> 00:04:04,270
It was a plume about three
hundred kilometers high.

80
00:04:04,440 --> 00:04:06,640
A volcano, erupting.

81
00:04:06,820 --> 00:04:10,590
The first active volcano ever
found beyond Earth.

82
00:04:10,720 --> 00:04:14,300
Scientists had expected Io to be a
dead, cratered moon.

83
00:04:14,480 --> 00:04:18,240
Instead, it was the most active
world they had ever seen.

84
00:04:18,300 --> 00:04:22,010
Next came Saturn, in November
nineteen eighty.

85
00:04:22,060 --> 00:04:24,770
Here, Voyager 1 faced a choice.

86
00:04:24,900 --> 00:04:28,800
Saturn's biggest moon, Titan, has
a thick atmosphere,

87
00:04:28,820 --> 00:04:31,560
and scientists badly wanted a close look.

88
00:04:31,580 --> 00:04:36,000
But flying past Titan would bend Voyager
1's path up and out of the plane

89
00:04:36,020 --> 00:04:37,320
of the planets.

90
00:04:37,340 --> 00:04:38,440
No Uranus.

91
00:04:38,460 --> 00:04:39,480
No Neptune.

92
00:04:39,500 --> 00:04:40,820
They chose Titan.

93
00:04:40,840 --> 00:04:43,940
Voyager 1's tour of the planets
ended right there.

94
00:04:43,960 --> 00:04:46,660
So the grand tour fell to Voyager 2.

95
00:04:46,680 --> 00:04:49,900
It reached Uranus in January
nineteen eighty-six,

96
00:04:49,920 --> 00:04:52,840
and Neptune in August
nineteen eighty-nine.

97
00:04:52,860 --> 00:04:56,920
At Neptune, it measured the strongest
winds in the solar system,

98
00:04:56,940 --> 00:05:01,440
and on its moon Triton, it caught dark
plumes shooting up from the ice,

99
00:05:01,460 --> 00:05:02,740
like geysers.

100
00:05:02,760 --> 00:05:07,800
It is still the only spacecraft that has ever
visited either of these planets.

101
00:05:07,820 --> 00:05:12,120
Every close-up picture of those two worlds
comes from a few days of Voyager

102
00:05:12,140 --> 00:05:13,840
2 flying past.

103
00:05:13,860 --> 00:05:18,240
By nineteen ninety, Voyager 1 was about
six billion kilometers from the

104
00:05:18,260 --> 00:05:18,960
Sun.

105
00:05:18,980 --> 00:05:23,540
Carl Sagan pushed for one last
picture: turn the camera around,

106
00:05:23,560 --> 00:05:24,920
and photograph home.

107
00:05:24,940 --> 00:05:28,460
On February fourteenth,
nineteen ninety, it did.

108
00:05:28,480 --> 00:05:32,460
In that picture, Earth is smaller
than a single pixel.

109
00:05:32,480 --> 00:05:36,580
A pale blue dot, caught in a beam
of scattered sunlight.

110
00:05:36,600 --> 00:05:40,860
Thirty-four minutes later, Voyager 1's
cameras were switched off for good,

111
00:05:40,880 --> 00:05:43,360
to save power for the long road ahead.

112
00:05:43,380 --> 00:05:46,880
The Sun blows a constant wind of
charged particles.

113
00:05:46,900 --> 00:05:50,000
That wind carves out a huge bubble
around the planets,

114
00:05:50,020 --> 00:05:51,500
called the heliosphere.

115
00:05:51,520 --> 00:05:55,087
Where the bubble ends,
interstellar space begins.

116
00:05:55,107 --> 00:05:58,980
In August twenty-twelve, Voyager
1 crossed that edge.

117
00:05:59,000 --> 00:06:01,267
But nobody could say so for sure.

118
00:06:01,287 --> 00:06:05,080
The instrument built to measure the solar
wind directly had stopped working

119
00:06:05,100 --> 00:06:06,540
back in nineteen eighty.

120
00:06:06,560 --> 00:06:10,300
It took more than a year, and a burst of
activity from the Sun that made

121
00:06:10,320 --> 00:06:15,000
the gas around Voyager ring like a bell,
before scientists were confident.

122
00:06:15,020 --> 00:06:18,160
Voyager 1 was in interstellar space.

123
00:06:18,180 --> 00:06:21,280
Voyager 2 followed in November
twenty-eighteen.

124
00:06:21,300 --> 00:06:25,040
They are the only working spacecraft that
have ever measured interstellar

125
00:06:25,060 --> 00:06:26,320
space directly.

126
00:06:26,340 --> 00:06:28,840
Out there, there is no map.

127
00:06:28,860 --> 00:06:30,700
Voyager is drawing it.

128
00:06:30,720 --> 00:06:34,080
Fixing a spacecraft you can
never touch is hard.

129
00:06:34,100 --> 00:06:38,940
Fixing one that is almost a full day of light
travel away is something else.

130
00:06:38,960 --> 00:06:43,740
In July twenty twenty-three, a wrong
command tilted Voyager 2's antenna

131
00:06:43,760 --> 00:06:45,740
two degrees away from Earth.

132
00:06:45,760 --> 00:06:48,540
Two degrees, and it went silent.

133
00:06:48,560 --> 00:06:52,567
The giant dish in Canberra, Australia,
sent its strongest signal,

134
00:06:52,587 --> 00:06:56,487
an interstellar shout, telling the
spacecraft to turn back.

135
00:06:56,507 --> 00:07:00,280
The message took about eighteen and
a half hours to arrive.

136
00:07:00,300 --> 00:07:02,360
Voyager 2 turned home.

137
00:07:02,380 --> 00:07:06,290
A few months later, Voyager 1
started sending gibberish.

138
00:07:06,640 --> 00:07:09,350
One memory chip in its
computer had failed.

139
00:07:09,520 --> 00:07:13,510
Nobody can replace a chip twenty-four
billion kilometers away.

140
00:07:13,680 --> 00:07:16,700
So the team moved the code
around the dead chip,

141
00:07:16,720 --> 00:07:21,250
piece by piece, because no single free
spot was big enough to hold it.

142
00:07:21,420 --> 00:07:25,730
In April twenty twenty-four, Voyager 1
started making sense again.

143
00:07:25,920 --> 00:07:29,780
And in twenty twenty-five, engineers
brought back a set of thrusters that

144
00:07:29,800 --> 00:07:32,790
had been considered dead since
two thousand four.

145
00:07:32,960 --> 00:07:36,340
They gambled, switched them
on, and it worked.

146
00:07:36,520 --> 00:07:39,070
Every one of those repairs
went the same way.

147
00:07:39,360 --> 00:07:43,590
Send a command, then wait almost two
days to find out if it worked.

148
00:07:43,980 --> 00:07:46,290
So, why don't we just switch it off?

149
00:07:46,560 --> 00:07:50,230
Each Voyager loses about four watts
of power every year.

150
00:07:50,400 --> 00:07:55,300
To keep them going, the team turns things
off one by one: heaters first,

151
00:07:55,320 --> 00:07:56,470
then instruments.

152
00:07:56,600 --> 00:08:00,980
On Voyager 1, an instrument that had been
running since nineteen seventy-seven

153
00:08:01,000 --> 00:08:04,000
was switched off in April
twenty twenty-six.

154
00:08:04,020 --> 00:08:08,160
But the two Voyagers are still the only
spacecraft taking measurements in

155
00:08:08,180 --> 00:08:09,830
interstellar space.

156
00:08:09,880 --> 00:08:13,540
Every extra year is data that
nobody else can collect,

157
00:08:13,560 --> 00:08:16,490
and a new probe would need
decades just to get there.

158
00:08:16,660 --> 00:08:18,670
So nobody is switching it off.

159
00:08:18,800 --> 00:08:22,150
The team keeps it alive for as
long as the power lasts.

160
00:08:22,480 --> 00:08:26,940
One day, the power will run out, and
Voyager will go quiet on its own.

161
00:08:27,100 --> 00:08:28,210
But it won't stop.

162
00:08:28,340 --> 00:08:33,200
In about forty thousand years, Voyager 1 will
pass less than two light-years

163
00:08:33,220 --> 00:08:36,020
from a star called Gliese four four five.

164
00:08:36,040 --> 00:08:38,000
Nobody will be listening by then.

165
00:08:38,020 --> 00:08:41,720
But it will still be carrying our
music, our pictures,

166
00:08:41,740 --> 00:08:43,090
and our hello.

